===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱewh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|chirche}}, from {{inh|en|ang|ċiriċe||church}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*kirikā}}, an early borrowing of {{der|en|grc|κυριακόν}}, neuter form of {{m|grc|κυριακός||belonging to the lord}}, from {{m|grc|κύριος||ruler, lord}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*ḱewh₁-||to swell, spread out, be strong, prevail}}.{{rel-top|additional etymological information}} For vowel evolution, see {{m|en|bury}}. {{m+|grc|κυριακόν}} was used of houses of Christian worship since circa 300 {{CE}}, especially in the East, though it was less common in this sense than {{m|grc|ἐκκλησία||congregation}} or {{m|grc|βασιλική||royal thing}}. An example of the direct Greek-to-Germanic progress of many Christian words, possibly via the {{w|Goths}}; it was probably used by West Germanic people in their pre-Christian period. Cognate with {{cog|sco|kirk||church}}, {{cog|fy|tsjerke||church}}, {{cog|stq|Säärke||church}}, {{cog|nl|kerk||church}}, {{cog|de|Kirche||church}}, {{cog|da|kirke||church}}, {{cog|sv|kyrka||church}}, {{cog|nb|kirke}}, {{cog|nn|kyrkje||church}}, and {{cog|is|kirkja||church}}. Also picked up by [[Slavic]], via {{cog|goh|chirihha}} (compare {{cog|cu|црькꙑ}}, {{cog|bg|църква}}, {{cog|ru|це́рковь}}). [[w:Romance languages|Romance]] and [[w:Celtic languages|Celtic]] languages use descendants of Latin {{m|la|ecclēsia}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

